=== lesson
id: algo-insertion
title: Insertion sort
summary: Insertion sort works the way many people sort a hand of cards: it takes the next item and slides it left into its place among the items already sorted, shifting the bigger ones one place right. You'll trace it, write it, see why it's O(n²) in the worst case but close to O(n) on lists that are nearly sorted, and why it's stable.

=== learn
### Sorting cards in your hand
To sort a hand of cards, you can pick up one card at a time and slide it into its place among the cards you already hold, which are in order. **Insertion sort** does the same. The front of the list is the **sorted part**. Each pass takes the next item, called the **key**, and inserts it into the sorted part: every bigger item moves one place right to make room. Here's 4 being inserted into the sorted part `[2, 5, 8]`:

| step | sorted part | what happens |
| --- | --- | --- |
| take the key | `[2, 5, 8]` | key = 4, and its old place is free |
| 8 > 4 | `[2, 5, _, 8]` | 8 shifts one place right |
| 5 > 4 | `[2, _, 5, 8]` | 5 shifts one place right |
| 2 < 4 | `[2, 4, 5, 8]` | stop, and put 4 in the free place |

The shifting stops at the first item that isn't bigger than the key, or at the front of the list, and the key goes into the free place.

### Pass after pass
At the start, the sorted part is only the first item: one item on its own is always in order. Pass 1 inserts the item at index 1, pass 2 the item at index 2, until the last item has been inserted. Here's `[5, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3]`:

| pass | key | shifted right | list after the pass |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 2 | 5 | [2, 5, 4, 6, 1, 3] |
| 2 | 4 | 5 | [2, 4, 5, 6, 1, 3] |
| 3 | 6 | nothing | [2, 4, 5, 6, 1, 3] |
| 4 | 1 | 6, 5, 4, 2 | [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 3] |
| 5 | 3 | 6, 5, 4 | [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] |

After pass k, the first k + 1 items are in order. Unlike selection sort, they aren't necessarily in their final places: the 2, placed first in pass 1, moves right again when the 1 is inserted in pass 4. A list of n items takes n − 1 passes.

### The code
`key` keeps a copy of the item being inserted, because the first shift writes over its place. `j` walks left through the sorted part:
```python
def insertion_sort(items):
    for i in range(1, len(items)):
        key = items[i]
        j = i - 1
        while j >= 0 and items[j] > key:
            items[j + 1] = items[j]
            j -= 1
        items[j + 1] = key
    return items

print(insertion_sort([5, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3]), insertion_sort([3, 1, 2]), insertion_sort([]))
```
```output
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] [1, 2, 3] []
```
`items[j + 1] = items[j]` copies a bigger item one place right. The loop stops when `j` reaches −1, past the front of the list, or when `items[j]` isn't bigger than the key. Either way, the free place is `j + 1`, so that's where the key goes. `j >= 0` is tested first, so `items[j]` is only read when j is a real index of the sorted part. Animation 1 runs this code.

### Counting the work
Each shift moves one bigger item past the key, which puts one pair that was in the wrong order right. So insertion sort makes exactly as many shifts as the list has inversions, the same number as bubble sort's swaps. In the worst case, a list in reverse order, the key at index i shifts all i items before it: 1 + 2 + … + (n − 1) = n × (n − 1) / 2 shifts, which is O(n²). In the best case, a sorted list, each key is compared once with its left neighbour and nothing shifts: n − 1 comparisons, O(n).
```python
def shifts(items):
    count = 0
    for i in range(1, len(items)):
        key = items[i]
        j = i - 1
        while j >= 0 and items[j] > key:
            items[j + 1] = items[j]
            j -= 1
            count += 1
        items[j + 1] = key
    return count

print(shifts([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]), shifts([5, 4, 3, 2, 1]), shifts([2, 1, 3, 5, 4]))
```
```output
0 10 2
```
The extra space is O(1): only `key`, `i` and `j`. Animation 2 compares a nearly sorted list with a reversed one.

### Nearly sorted lists
The best case isn't only for lists that are already sorted. Each key shifts only past the bigger items in front of it, so a list where every item is close to its final place sorts in close to n steps. If no item is more than k places from where it belongs, there are at most about n × k shifts: O(nk), which is O(n) when k is a small fixed number.

| a list of 1,000 items | shifts |
| --- | --- |
| sorted | 0 |
| sorted, with one new item added at the end | at most 999 |
| sorted, then each pair of neighbours swapped: [2, 1, 4, 3, …] | 500 |
| reversed | 499,500 |

That's why insertion sort is used for short lists, for nearly sorted ones, and for adding a few new items to a list that's already sorted. Python's own `sorted()` uses insertion sort on short stretches of the list before it merges them together.

### A stable sort
The shifting stops at the first item that isn't bigger than the key: the test is `items[j] > key`. An item equal to the key isn't bigger, so the key stops right after it and never passes it. Equal items keep their order: insertion sort is **stable**.
```python
tasks = [("write", 2), ("test", 1), ("plan", 2), ("ship", 1)]
for i in range(1, len(tasks)):
    key = tasks[i]
    j = i - 1
    while j >= 0 and tasks[j][1] > key[1]:
        tasks[j + 1] = tasks[j]
        j -= 1
    tasks[j + 1] = key
print(tasks)
```
```output
[('test', 1), ('ship', 1), ('write', 2), ('plan', 2)]
```
Sorted by priority, "test" stays before "ship", and "write" before "plan", as in the original list. With `>=` instead of `>`, equal items would shift too, and "ship" would end up before "test".

### Three simple sorts
| measure | bubble sort | selection sort | insertion sort |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| sorted list | O(n), with the early stop | O(n²) | O(n) |
| worst case | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(n²) |
| moves items by | swapping neighbours | one long swap per pass | shifting right |
| stable | yes | no | yes |

All three are O(n²) in the worst case. In practice insertion sort is usually the fastest of them: it does little work on nearly sorted data, and a shift writes one place where a swap writes two. To sort big lists quickly, you need a different idea. The next lesson, merge sort, is the first O(n log n) sort.

=== keypoints
- Insertion sort grows a sorted part at the front. Each pass takes the next item, the key, and shifts the bigger items of the sorted part one place right, then puts the key in the free place.
- `for i in range(1, len(items)):`, `key = items[i]`, `j = i - 1`, then `while j >= 0 and items[j] > key:` shift with `items[j + 1] = items[j]` and `j -= 1`. Finally `items[j + 1] = key`.
- After pass k, the first k + 1 items are in order, but not necessarily in their final places.
- Shifts = inversions. Worst case (reversed list): n × (n − 1) / 2 shifts, O(n²). Best case (sorted list): n − 1 comparisons, O(n). Nearly sorted lists are fast. O(1) extra space.
- Stable: it shifts only items strictly bigger than the key.

=== example: Sorting a hand of cards
intro: Five cards are insertion sorted. After each pass, the program prints the key it inserted, the hand, and how many cards shifted to make room.
```python
hand = [7, 3, 9, 1, 5]
for i in range(1, len(hand)):
    key = hand[i]
    j = i - 1
    shifts = 0
    while j >= 0 and hand[j] > key:
        hand[j + 1] = hand[j]
        j -= 1
        shifts += 1
    hand[j + 1] = key
    print("inserted", key, hand, "shifts:", shifts)
```
1: Five cards, in no order. The 7 on its own is the sorted part.
2-3: Each pass picks up the next card as the key.
4-9: Bigger cards in the sorted part shift right, one at a time, counting the shifts.
10: The key goes into the free place.
11: 9 needs no shifts, because it's bigger than every card before it. The 1 shifts all three cards in front of it.

=== example: Keeping scores sorted as they arrive
intro: Insertion sort can sort items as they arrive, one at a time. Each new score goes on the end of a list that's already sorted, then moves left into its place, so the list is sorted after every step.
```python
def insert_sorted(scores, new):
    scores.append(new)
    j = len(scores) - 2
    while j >= 0 and scores[j] > new:
        scores[j + 1] = scores[j]
        j -= 1
    scores[j + 1] = new

scores = []
for s in [52, 87, 61, 95, 61]:
    insert_sorted(scores, s)
    print(scores)
```
1-2: The new score is appended, which makes the list one place longer, with room for a shift.
3: `j` starts at the last score before the new one.
4-7: The usual insertion: bigger scores shift right, then the new score goes into the free place.
9-12: The list is sorted after every insert. Each insert costs at most one shift per score already in the list: O(n).
12: The second 61 goes after the first one: equal scores stay in the order they arrived.

=== animation: Insertion sort, key by key
view: bars items ptr=i,j range=0:i hit=j+1
vars key i j
intro: Each bar is an item. The bars up to `i` are the part being sorted; the dim bars haven't been reached yet. The key is held in `key` while bigger items shift right, and the yellow bar is the free place where the key will go. Until the key is written, that place still shows a copy of the item that shifted out of it.
```python
def insertion_sort(items):
    for i in range(1, len(items)):
        key = items[i]
        j = i - 1
        while j >= 0 and items[j] > key:
            items[j + 1] = items[j]
            j -= 1
        items[j + 1] = key
    return items

insertion_sort([5, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3])
```
1: Defines insertion_sort. Nothing runs yet.
2: Next pass: i is the index of the next item to insert. After the last one, the loop ends.
3: Pick up the key, {items[i]}.
4: Start j one place left of the key, at index {i - 1}.
5: {'j is ' + str(j) + ': the front of the list has been passed.' if j < 0 else 'Is ' + str(items[j]) + ' bigger than the key, ' + str(key) + '? ' + str(items[j] > key) + '.'}
6: Shift {items[j]} one place right.
7: Move j one place left.
8: Put the key, {key}, into the free place at index {j + 1}.
9: Sorted: {items}.
11: Sort six numbers.

=== animation: Nearly sorted or reversed
view: bars items ptr=i,j range=0:i hit=j+1
vars key i j shifts
intro: The same insertion sort, counting its shifts, first on a list where only two neighbours are out of order, then on a list in reverse order. The nearly sorted list needs 1 shift; the reversed list needs a shift at every comparison.
```python
def insertion_sort(items):
    shifts = 0
    for i in range(1, len(items)):
        key = items[i]
        j = i - 1
        while j >= 0 and items[j] > key:
            items[j + 1] = items[j]
            j -= 1
            shifts += 1
        items[j + 1] = key
    return shifts

insertion_sort([1, 2, 4, 3, 5])
insertion_sort([4, 3, 2, 1])
```
1: Defines insertion_sort. Nothing runs yet.
2: No shifts yet.
3: Next pass, if there is one.
4: Pick up the key, {items[i]}.
5: Start j at index {i - 1}.
6: {'j is ' + str(j) + ': past the front.' if j < 0 else 'Is ' + str(items[j]) + ' bigger than ' + str(key) + '? ' + str(items[j] > key) + '.'}
7: Shift {items[j]} right.
8: Move j left.
9: One more shift.
10: Put {key} at index {j + 1}.
11: Sorted, with {shifts} shifts.
13: First, a list where only 4 and 3 are out of order.
14: Then a list in reverse order.

=== task: predict easy
title: Inserting one item
code:
```python
items = [2, 5, 8, 4]
key = items[3]
j = 2
while j >= 0 and items[j] > key:
    items[j + 1] = items[j]
    j -= 1
    print(items)
items[j + 1] = key
print(items, j)
```
wrong[[2, 5, 4, 8]\n[2, 4, 5, 8]\n[2, 4, 5, 8] 0]: A shift copies an item one place right; it doesn't swap. Until the key is written, the list holds two copies of the item that was shifted last.
wrong[[2, 5, 8, 8]\n[2, 5, 5, 8]\n[2, 4, 5, 8] 1]: j stops at 0, the index of 2, the first item that isn't bigger than the key. The key goes one place to the right of it.
wrong[[2, 5, 8, 8]\n[2, 5, 5, 8]\n[2, 5, 5, 8]\n[2, 4, 5, 8] 0]: 2 isn't bigger than 4, so the loop stops after two shifts, and the print inside it runs twice.
hint: `items[j + 1] = items[j]` copies the item at j into the next place. Nothing is written into index j.
hint: The loop stops when it reaches an item that isn't bigger than 4.
right: 8 is copied right, then 5, so the list shows a repeated item until the last line writes 4 into index 1. j stops at 0.
wrong: Shift 8 (the list is [2, 5, 8, 8]), then 5 ([2, 5, 5, 8]). 2 isn't bigger than 4, so the loop stops with j = 0, and 4 goes in at index 1.

=== task: mcq easy
title: Which items shift?
prompt: Insertion sort has sorted `[3, 6, 9]` and now inserts the key 7. Which items shift one place right?
* Only 9 :: Right. 9 is bigger than 7 and shifts; 6 isn't bigger, so the shifting stops there.
- 9 and 6 :: 6 is smaller than 7, so it stays, and the shifting stops at it.
- 3, 6 and 9 :: Only items bigger than the key shift.
- None of them :: 9 is bigger than 7, so it has to make room.
hint: The shifting starts at the right end of the sorted part and moves left.
hint: It stops at the first item that isn't bigger than the key.
right: The scan goes right to left: 9 > 7 shifts, 6 > 7 is False, so it stops. 7 goes between 6 and 9.
wrong: Walk left from 9: 9 is bigger than 7, so it shifts. 6 isn't, so the shifting stops and 7 goes right after 6.

=== task: cells easy
title: Pass by pass
prompt: Insertion sort runs on `[4, 3, 1, 2]`. Fill in the list after each pass.
labels: after pass 1 | after pass 2 | after pass 3
answer: [3, 4, 1, 2] | [1, 3, 4, 2] | [1, 2, 3, 4]
wrong[[1, 3, 4, 2] | [1, 2, 4, 3] | [1, 2, 3, 4]]: That's selection sort. Insertion sort's pass 1 inserts only the item at index 1, 3, into the sorted part [4].
wrong[[3, 4, 1, 2] | [3, 1, 4, 2] | [1, 2, 3, 4]]: In pass 2, the key 1 shifts past both 4 and 3, all the way to the front: [1, 3, 4, 2].
wrong[[3, 4, 1, 2] | [1, 3, 4, 2] | [1, 3, 2, 4]]: In pass 3, the key 2 shifts past 4 and 3, and stops at 1: [1, 2, 3, 4].
hint: Pass k inserts the item at index k into the sorted part in front of it.
hint: The key moves left past every bigger item.
right: Pass 1 puts 3 before 4, pass 2 moves 1 to the front, and pass 3 puts 2 between 1 and 3.
wrong: Pass 1: key 3 shifts 4: [3, 4, 1, 2]. Pass 2: key 1 shifts 4 and 3: [1, 3, 4, 2]. Pass 3: key 2 shifts 4 and 3: [1, 2, 3, 4].

=== task: code easy
title: Write insertion sort
prompt: Write `insertion_sort(items)` that sorts the list from smallest to largest in place with insertion sort, and returns it. The speed check uses lists that are already sorted, which insertion sort handles in O(n). Don't use `sorted` or `.sort()`.
starter:
```python
def insertion_sort(items):
    # your code here
    return items
```
solution:
```python
def insertion_sort(items):
    for i in range(1, len(items)):
        key = items[i]
        j = i - 1
        while j >= 0 and items[j] > key:
            items[j + 1] = items[j]
            j -= 1
        items[j + 1] = key
    return items
```
tests:
- insertion_sort([5, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3])
- insertion_sort([])
- insertion_sort([1])
- insertion_sort([3, 3, 1])
- insertion_sort([9, 8, 7, 6])
- insertion_sort([-1, 5, -3])
speed: setup=items = list(range(n)) | call=insertion_sort(items) | target=O(n)
slow:
```python
def insertion_sort(items):
    for i in range(1, len(items)):
        key = items[i]
        p = 0
        while p < i and items[p] <= key:
            p += 1
        for j in range(i, p, -1):
            items[j] = items[j - 1]
        items[p] = key
    return items
```
forbid[sorted(]: Write the passes yourself instead of calling `sorted`.
forbid[.sort(]: Write the passes yourself instead of calling `.sort()`.
fail[1]: The sorted list is [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].
fail[4]: Equal items end up next to each other: [1, 3, 3].
fail[6]: Negative numbers sort too: [-3, -1, 5].
error[IndexError]: Shift with `items[j + 1] = items[j]` only while `j >= 0`, and put the key at `j + 1`.
hint: For each i from 1, save `key = items[i]` and start `j = i - 1`.
hint: `while j >= 0 and items[j] > key:` shift `items[j + 1] = items[j]` and do `j -= 1`.
hint: After the loop, `items[j + 1] = key`. Search from the right end of the sorted part: on a sorted list, the loop then stops at once.
right: Each key walks left only past bigger items, so a sorted list costs one comparison per key: O(n). A reversed list is the worst case, O(n²).
wrong: Save the key, shift bigger items of the sorted part one place right, starting next to the key and moving left, then write the key into the free place.

