=== task: multi medium
title: When insertion beats selection
prompt: Choose **every** situation where insertion sort does less work than selection sort.
* The list is already sorted :: Right. Insertion sort makes n − 1 comparisons; selection sort still makes n × (n − 1) / 2.
* Only a few items are out of place :: Right. Insertion sort's work grows with the number of pairs out of order, and here there are few.
* A few new items are added to the end of a sorted list :: Right. Each new item shifts only past the bigger items in front of it.
- The list is in reverse order :: Both make n × (n − 1) / 2 comparisons, and insertion sort shifts at every one of them, while selection sort makes at most n − 1 swaps.
- Moving an item is far more expensive than comparing two :: Selection sort's at most n − 1 swaps win there; insertion sort can shift O(n²) times.
hint: Insertion sort's work depends on how unsorted the list is. Selection sort's doesn't.
hint: Which sort moves items less often in the worst case?
right: Insertion sort wins whenever the list is sorted or close to it. Selection sort wins when moving items is what costs the most, because it moves each item at most once.
wrong: Selection sort always scans everything, so on sorted or nearly sorted lists insertion sort does far less. But on a reversed list, insertion sort moves items O(n²) times, and selection sort only O(n) times.

